1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.100
So you just
saw a sample of the kinds of unreliable

2
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.100
LLM behaviors
that can affect your proof of concept

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.500
genAI application. In this lesson,
you'll take a deeper

4
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:14.733
look at how AI guardrails can help
you mitigate these problems

5
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.433
by validating and verifying everything
your application does.

6
00:00:18.933 --> 00:00:21.933
The first question we have is
what are guardrails?

7
00:00:22.333 --> 00:00:25.900
Guardrails are secondary
checks or validations

8
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.933
that ensure that inputs or outputs

9
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.200
of an LLM call is as expected.

10
00:00:32.433 --> 00:00:35.300
When you're looking at
like what an expectation might be,

11
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.000
you might be checking,
you know, something very,

12
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.200
very trivial like "Is my LLM output
formatted correctly?

13
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:42.900
If it's like a simple string, does it,

14
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.400
is it split into,
you know, like a list or something?"

15
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.600
Or if it's, you know,
maybe a json output, like, does

16
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.600
it look like a json output of the schema
that I expect?

17
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.100
Or it might end up being like a very,
very complex expectation, right?

18
00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:57.500
Which is making sure that your
AI output isn't hallucinated

19
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.500
and there are no jailbreak attempts
that are detected.

20
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:05.600
But essentially it's not blindly trusting
the LLM to do the right thing, but explicitly

21
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:10.400
verifying whatever your expectation
or whatever your success criteria

22
00:01:10.433 --> 00:01:15.400
out of that LLM might be. The previous lesson
looked at your standard RAG chatbot.

23
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:19.433
If now you want to take that RAG chatbot
to a production-ready stage,

24
00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.700
where would you go ahead and apply your
AI guardrails?

25
00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.233
So on the left, what we have
is a very standard, straightforward

26
00:01:27.533 --> 00:01:31.700
barebones of an LLM call,
where what you have fundamentally is,

27
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.400
you know, you start with the prompt

28
00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:36.400
and if you're doing RAG or something
complicated,

29
00:01:36.533 --> 00:01:37.500
your retrievals

30
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.633
from your vector database
and your system prompt,

31
00:01:39.633 --> 00:01:42.000
and everything else
goes into this little box here.

32
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:42.800
Right.

33
00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:45.900
And then once you have this, this prompt,
you send it over to your LLM.

34
00:01:46.233 --> 00:01:49.800
Your LLM, you know,
like do some nice next-token prediction

35
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.000
and then get an output
which you will end up

36
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.800
sending back to the rest of your genAI
application. Right?

37
00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:57.033
Now the core

38
00:01:57.033 --> 00:02:01.033
idea behind guardrails is very,
very straightforward, which is once again

39
00:02:01.033 --> 00:02:04.700
explicitly
verifying whatever your LLM does.

40
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:10.100
So now before you send your prompt
over to your LLM, what you would do

41
00:02:10.533 --> 00:02:15.533
is send this prompt over
to force this explicit input verification

42
00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:20.200
sweep our input guard.
Where that guard contains

43
00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:24.200
a number of guardrails that explicitly
validate your expectations.

44
00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:24.733
Right?

45
00:02:24.733 --> 00:02:27.733
So once again these expectations
can be a lot of different things

46
00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:29.533
depending on what you're building.

47
00:02:29.533 --> 00:02:31.300
Maybe your expectation is

48
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:35.500
if my prompt contains any PII,
I don't want to send it over.

49
00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:36.000
Right?

50
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.833
So that is an expectation that you can
explicitly check for using a guardrail.

51
00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.900
Maybe you want to check
like is this question or is this prompt

52
00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:48.500
something that is off-topic
and not what my chatbot is intended for?

53
00:02:48.933 --> 00:02:50.700
Maybe even something
more sophisticated, like,

54
00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.800
is there a jailbreak attempt
that is detected in my prompt?

55
00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.933
And once you know that your prompt doesn't
contain sources

56
00:02:57.933 --> 00:03:02.033
of unreliable behavior, that will in turn
make your application unreliable.

57
00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.100
Let's say
you send that prompt over to your LLM

58
00:03:05.133 --> 00:03:09.200
once it passes through this verification
suite, your LLM does it's thing

59
00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.166
and then ends up getting an output,

60
00:03:11.166 --> 00:03:15.400
which once again, you will first pass
through to this explicit

61
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:21.000
output verification suite or output guard,
which contains a number of guardrails

62
00:03:21.033 --> 00:03:25.200
that check for different types of criteria
that you may have. On the output side,

63
00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.033
maybe something else
that you care about is like,

64
00:03:27.033 --> 00:03:29.600
are there any hallucinations
that are present in my text? Right?

65
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:31.433
So that's something you can test for.

66
00:03:31.433 --> 00:03:34.000
Are there once again off-topic responses

67
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.000
or are there
maybe profanity or unsafe outputs.

68
00:03:37.466 --> 00:03:41.633
So you can explicitly test for
any of these types of criteria failures.

69
00:03:41.666 --> 00:03:44.366
So how does a guardrail
really work under the hood?

70
00:03:44.366 --> 00:03:47.866
They might be something
as simple as a rules engine,

71
00:03:47.866 --> 00:03:50.600
which might contain, let's say,
regex rules. Right?

72
00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:54.666
Is there this type of text
that's present in my LLM output?

73
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.333
They might end up being something
more sophisticated than that.

74
00:03:57.333 --> 00:04:00.566
Where they're small,
fine-tuned machine learning models

75
00:04:00.566 --> 00:04:03.600
that are actually running
within the process of your guardrail.

76
00:04:03.933 --> 00:04:07.533
Maybe they're looking at named entities
that are present in

77
00:04:07.533 --> 00:04:09.600
any text that you pass into the guardrail.

78
00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.400
Maybe there are specific topics or profane
outputs, etc.

79
00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:16.400
that are present that, you know,
you're checking the fine-tuned ML model.

80
00:04:17.066 --> 00:04:20.900
Some guardrails might also end up
being secondary LLM calls.

81
00:04:21.366 --> 00:04:25.500
So you know, you might end up seeing a lot
like "score this output on how well

82
00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:26.966
it answers the user's questions"

83
00:04:26.966 --> 00:04:29.733
or "how much it aligns with,
you know, some rules that you have." Right?

84
00:04:29.733 --> 00:04:32.733
So that might be done
within the scope of a guardrail.

85
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.133
Very commonly, though, a guardrail
might end up being a combination of all

86
00:04:37.133 --> 00:04:41.166
of these above technologies where you can
mix and match what tools you use.

87
00:04:41.333 --> 00:04:45.100
So then you can very accurately capture
the behavior that you want

88
00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:46.833
from your LLM application.

89
00:04:46.833 --> 00:04:48.700
So with this lesson,
we looked at a lot of you know,

90
00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:51.133
what guardrails are, how they really work.

91
00:04:51.133 --> 00:04:53.800
But let's dive back to our first lesson
where we really look at,

92
00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:57.866
you know, how do guardrails help mitigate
a lot of the unreliable behavior

93
00:04:57.866 --> 00:05:00.866
that exists in modern generative
AI applications?

94
00:05:01.233 --> 00:05:05.333
The first way they help
is that they can explicitly verify

95
00:05:05.333 --> 00:05:09.766
what are the inviolable constraints
that are too high

96
00:05:09.766 --> 00:05:14.633
cost for your system to, you know,
get wrong, such as never leak PII, right?

97
00:05:14.633 --> 00:05:18.500
There's actual financial fines
and risks for leaking

98
00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:20.300
PII in the system that shouldn't.

99
00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:21.200
And then guardrails

100
00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.400
can make sure that, you know,
you're not just relying on your AI system.

101
00:05:24.666 --> 00:05:27.700
You're explicitly verifying
that that inviolable

102
00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:30.700
constraint of not leaking
PII is never violated.

103
00:05:31.033 --> 00:05:34.233
Second is that even a few guardrails
are running along with their system.

104
00:05:34.533 --> 00:05:38.533
They can really measure the occurrence
of undesirable behavior in your system,

105
00:05:38.866 --> 00:05:41.033
such as,
you know how many times has my LLM

106
00:05:41.033 --> 00:05:44.266
refused to answer a question,
or how many times my LLM, you know,

107
00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:47.166
get questions about like specific topics
in my system, right?

108
00:05:47.166 --> 00:05:50.166
So it's taking this idea of something
that's very hard to measure,

109
00:05:50.433 --> 00:05:54.600
which is, you know, how well does my chatbot
help resolve my customer questions

110
00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:57.600
and then really break that down
into specific

111
00:05:57.633 --> 00:06:01.700
small checks that give you an idea
of the overall performance of that system.

112
00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:05.533
The third key technique,
especially necessary for as we move

113
00:06:05.533 --> 00:06:10.533
towards agentic and multi-step, complex
applications, is that you can contain

114
00:06:11.066 --> 00:06:15.000
the cascading error that happens
in a lot of these multi-step applications,

115
00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:19.000
and make sure that you really
are almost drawing a bounding box around

116
00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:22.466
what that AI model can do,
so that as you combine

117
00:06:22.466 --> 00:06:26.600
a lot of these LLM calls in sequence,
you know that their error is contained

118
00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:28.766
and you don't get that
same compounding inside.

119
00:06:28.766 --> 00:06:30.800
And then using all of this, you know,
you really end up

120
00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:34.066
limiting the worst-case
risk for your GenAI application.

121
00:06:34.666 --> 00:06:37.833
So now that you understand
what guardrails are

122
00:06:37.833 --> 00:06:40.833
and how they increase
the reliability of your application,

123
00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:44.333
let's head on to the next lesson
and implement your first guardrail.

124
00:06:44.733 --> 00:06:48.233
This will be a very, very simple example
where we constrain the chatbot

125
00:06:48.233 --> 00:06:49.266
that we've been building

126
00:06:49.266 --> 00:06:53.566
to not reveal this exciting secret project
that the pizzeria has been working on.

